==English==

===Etymology===
From the {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|circā||about}} and {{m|la|diēs||a day}} with the {{etyl|en|-}} suffix {{m|en|-an}}. Compare {{m|en|circannual}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|biology}} of, relating to, or showing [[rhythmic]] behaviour with a [[period]] of 24 hours; especially of a [[biological]] process
#* '''2000''', Arnold Sameroff, ''et al''., ''Handbook of Developmental Psychopathology'', page 310
#*: The '''circadian''' clock is involved in the regulation of the diurnal sleep-wake cycle, […].
#* {{quote-text|lang=en|year=2002|author=Jill B. Becker|title=Behavioral Endocrinology|page=483|passage=To summarize, the '''circadian''' system, particularly the SCN, controls the '''circadian''' pattern of melatonin release in mammals.}}
#* {{quote-text|lang=en|year=2005|author=Paul Martin|title=Counting Sheep: The Science and Pleasures of Sleep and Dreams|page=114|passage=The most obvious '''circadian''' rhythm is the daily cycle of sleep and activity.}}
#* {{quote-journal|lang=en|title=Quite interesting: A quietly intriguing column from the brains behind QI, the BBC quiz show. This week; QI orchids you not|work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Weekend)|date=5 April 2014|page=W22|passage='''Circadian''' clocks developed early in evolution in single-celled organisms, before the animal and plant kingdom split from one another. The original clocks probably functioned to protect the cells from damage induced by high UV radiation.}}

